#### Task conventions

| Context | Tracker | Notes |
|---------|---------|-------|
| Vertical slices (`/to-issues`) | **Local markdown** under `.scratch/<feature-slug>/issues/` | `**Status:** ready-for-agent`; `OVERVIEW.md` when 2+ slices |
| Ad-hoc feature or bug (ClickUp) | ClickUp default list | Standard **Task** type (omit `task_type`); tags `ready-for-agent` when agent-ready; assignees `["93674717"]` |

**When creating ClickUp items**, use the standard Task type: omit `task_type` on `clickup_create_task`. Do not pass the literal value `"Task"` because the API may reject it when only custom task types are enumerated.

Tags must already exist in the space — confirm or ask before publishing.

### 📋 Implementation plans & PRDs (workspace path — required)
- ✅ **Always create/save plans in the workspace folder** `.cursor/plans/` so developers can see them in-repo immediately.
- ✅ **`/to-prd`** writes PRDs to `.cursor/plans/<feature-slug>.prd.md` only — **no tracker issues**. Use **`/to-issues`** to publish vertical slices under `.scratch/<feature-slug>/issues/`.
- ✅ If `CreatePlan` writes to `~/.cursor/plans/*.plan.md`, **immediately copy/sync** the same file into this repo at `.cursor/plans/<same-filename>.plan.md`.
- ✅ Keep the **same filename** as the Cursor plan; do not duplicate under a second name unless merging plans.
- ✅ When linking from ClickUp or PRs, use the **repo-relative path** `.cursor/plans/...`, not `~/.cursor/plans/...`.
- ✅ Commit the copied plan with the feature PR (or in a separate commit when the user asks to commit plans only).
- This is the **allowed exception** to “no random .md files”: plans/PRDs under `.cursor/plans/` (`*.plan.md`, `*.prd.md`), and vertical-slice issues under `.scratch/<feature-slug>/` — not new READMEs or ad-hoc docs elsewhere.

### 📋 Plan prompts — codebase scan & plan improvements (required)
Before finalizing any implementation plan (initial draft or after user feedback), **always scan the project** for additional files to update — do not rely only on the files the user named. See also `.cursor/rules/planning.mdc`.

**Codebase scan (required):**
- Search for the entity/field/API name, related types, enums, and similar existing patterns (`grep`, semantic search, and/or explore subagents in parallel).
- Trace the full stack: Prisma schema/migrations → API handlers → services → UI forms → list/grid → export/import → i18n (EN/HE) → tests → report metadata.
- For each candidate file, classify as **required**, **optional/out of scope**, or **no change needed** (with a one-line reason).
- Include a **“Codebase scan”** section in the plan listing all three buckets — not just the obvious files.
- Re-run the scan when the user asks to review the plan or says “what else needs updating?”; update the plan document accordingly.

**Suggest plan improvements (required):**
- Call out easy-to-miss touchpoints (e.g. manual export mappers, row/API types, sort allowlists, validation parity client/server, conditional required fields).
- Note incorrect assumptions (e.g. “export auto-picks grid columns” when a handler maps fields manually).
- Propose optional follow-ups (unit tests, report builder fields, edge cases) clearly labeled **out of scope unless requested**.
- Flag cross-cutting rules that affect the plan (translations, styling approval, migration safety).
- If the scan reveals gaps or better approaches, update the plan before asking the user to approve execution.

### Tooltips
- ✅ **Always place tooltips below the trigger** — use MUI `placement="bottom"` on every `Tooltip` (including shared helpers like `getRTLTooltipProps`). Do not default to `top`, `left`, or `right` unless the user explicitly requests another placement.
- ✅ Keep other tooltip props per `.cursor/rules/frontend-tooltips.mdc` (`arrow`, delays, RTL text direction in `PopperProps`).

### 🚫 Critical Rules
- ❌ NEVER create .md files, READMEs, or run commands without approval (except implementation plans under `.cursor/plans/` and `/to-issues` slices under `.scratch/` — see above)
- ❌ NEVER modify translation files without explicit permission
- ❌ NEVER add new styles (theme overrides, global CSS, component `sx` blocks, or new CSS classes) without explicit user approval — reuse existing theme tokens, MUI variants, and patterns first
- ❌ NEVER run `npx prisma db push --force-reset` or `npx prisma migrate dev`
- ❌ NEVER run `npm run dev` or any development server commands automatically
- ✅ Use `search_replace` with 3-5 lines context, `read_file`, `grep_search`
- ✅ Use static analysis: `npx tsc --noEmit`, `npm run test:unit`, `npm run lint`

### ❓ Clarifying & design questions (required format)

When a decision is genuinely the user's to make — planning, grilling (`/grill-me`), ambiguous requirements, or trade-offs the codebase cannot resolve — **always** use the **`AskQuestion` tool** (same format as Plan mode). Do **not** list options as A/B/C bullets or numbered lists in chat.

**Rules:**
- **One question per turn** — wait for the answer before asking the next.
- **Two or more concrete options** per question (Cursor also offers **Other** for free text).
- Put the **recommended option first** and append **`(Recommended)`** to its label.
- Keep option labels **short but specific** — include the outcome (e.g. KPI value or behavior), not just a name.
- Brief context **above** the question is fine; the **choices themselves** belong in `AskQuestion`, not inline in prose.
- If the answer is discoverable by reading the codebase, **explore first** — only ask when still blocked on a product/design choice.
- After the user answers, incorporate it into the plan, PRD, or next implementation step before continuing.

**Grilling (`/grill-me`, `/grilling`):** same rules — one `AskQuestion` per turn, recommended option first, no multi-question dumps. Use **simple language** (everyday words, short sentences, concrete examples when abstract; expand every acronym with its full name); see `.agents/skills/grill-me/SKILL.md`.

### 📚 Rule Files
All detailed rules are organized in `.cursor/rules/` directory for better maintainability and topic-specific reference.
